{"id": "reasoning-140183b2-4f09-5e45-a090-bd3e577d8365-0", "sessionId": "b9a80757-48a2-a90d-2f72-692761706b93", "turnId": "turn-8c7e0354138e253f073f636b837c66de", "createdAt": 1792345697617, "updatedAt": 1792345697617, "generationStatus": "completed", "relatedEntryId": null, "type": "reasoning", "text": "The `.venv` in the repo works: Python 3.13.9, numpy 2.4.3, scipy 1.16.3, pytest 9.0.1, anymaterial installed in site-packages, and anysolver resolves to the working tree `src/anysolver` (probably editable install). This is the environment previous workers used (the HANDOVER runs). \n\nNow I'll build the harness. Requirements:\n- Record exact command/cwd, input/source/test hashes, effective runtime versions/import origins/threadpool/JIT, exit/timeout, precise process-wall, cumulative used/remaining, counts, full stdout/stderr and JUnit/JSON.\n- evidence/<unique-attempt>/ under the task dir.\n- Budget ledger persists across sessions (JSON file in task dir).\n- Kill only own process tree on timeout.\n- One numerical child at a time.\n\nDesign: `harness.py` in task dir, invoked as:\n`python harness.py --name <unique-attempt> --timeout <s> -- <command...>`\nIt:\n1. Reads/creates `budget.json` (allowance 3600, reserve 900).\n2. Checks no other running child (lock file).\n3. Hashes the five source files + two tests + the script being run (input hashes).\n4. Runs subprocess with env vars (OMP_NUM_THREADS=1 etc., NUMBA_DISABLE_JIT=1, PYTHONPATH to src), measures process wall via time.perf_counter around subprocess.run with timeout; on timeout, kill process tree (psutil or taskkill /T /F).\n5. Writes evidence/<name>/: meta.json (command, cwd, hashes, runtime versions via a preflight import in the child? better: run a small preflight in the same subprocess? The assignment says \"Imports/setup/version preflight can be included in timed harness\"), stdout.txt, stderr.txt, exit code, wall seconds, cumulative budget.\n6. Appends to budget.json ledger.\n\nFor runtime versions/import origins: I can have the harness run a preflight python snippet in the child env before the main command but within the same timed attempt (as allowed). Simpler: the harness itself runs under the venv python, so it can import numpy/scipy/anysolver in-process... but that pollutes. Better: preflight as a subprocess `-c` command included in the timed attempt, then the main command. Actually simplest: harness runs the main command; separately (untimed, cheap) it records versions by running a tiny import probe. The requirement says preflight \"can be included in timed harness\" — optional. I'll include a preflight subprocess inside the attempt (timed), recording versions, import origins, threadpool info, JIT status. That's clean and matches \"Imports/setup/version preflight can be included in timed harness\".\n\nBudget accounting: budget.json with {\"allowance\": 3600, \"reserve\": 900, \"used\": X, \"attempts\": [...]}. Every attempt adds wall seconds. Persist immediately after each attempt.\n\nTimeout kill on Windows: use subprocess with timeout, then `taskkill /F /T /PID` or psutil. psutil may be installed in .venv? Let me not rely; use ctypes GenerateConsoleCtrlEvent? Simplest robust: `subprocess.run(..., timeout=t)` then on TimeoutExpired call `taskkill /PID <pid> /T /F` via subprocess. That kills the tree.\n\nOne child at a time: lock via a `running.lock` file with O_EXCL, removed in finally.\n\nNow the plan for deliverables and turn budget. I'm at turn ~12. I need to write checkpoint.md by turn 12! Let me count turns: each tool-call block is a turn. So far: 1 (todo+read), 2 (bash x2), 3 (read HANDOVER... actually turn 1 was todo+read_file HANDOVER (failed) + read global AGENTS). Let me count assistant messages with tool calls: \n1. todo + read HANDOVER + read AGENTS\n2. bash ls + bash ls\n3. read HANDOVER\n4. read philosophy + bash ls\n5. read SLICE3 + read identity + bash git\n6. bash sha256 + bash head ECOSYSTEM\n7. read ep_isolation\n8. read ep_sweep + converg