{"id": "reasoning-69d23a09-9303-5201-9134-3d722320ffb1-0", "sessionId": "b9a80757-48a2-a90d-2f72-692761706b93", "turnId": "turn-8c7e0354138e253f073f636b837c66de", "createdAt": 1792346330513, "updatedAt": 1792346330513, "generationStatus": "completed", "relatedEntryId": null, "type": "reasoning", "text": "Clean HEAD reproduces the candidate VK results exactly (lam 0.527868 / 0.466471, uz 0.004312 / -0.016745, same residuals). So the VK behavior is pre-existing on HEAD, identical under the candidate. Attribution complete:\n\nDeliverable 2 conclusions:\n1. Corotational plastic -6k/-10k/-14k: all stop at minimum_load_increment_reached with achieved lambda 0.9423/0.5650/0.4038; fz×lambda ≈ -5.65kN constant → plastic limit load; returned states are true equilibria at achieved lambda (independent rel residual ≤ 3.2e-8); residual at lambda=1 large as expected. No solver/reporting defect; no mixed trial/committed state; deep immutability verified. `converged` True for stopped_at_limit (HEAD-compatible).\n2. VK elastic/plastic -6k: identical on candidate and clean HEAD (same dependencies) → pre-existing behavior, not caused by Slices 1-3. VK elastic also stops at a partial lambda (0.5279) with a geometric limit point; the sign change of uz is part of that nonlinear path, and the reported state is a valid equilibrium at achieved lambda.\n\nClassification per assignment: partial load + small achieved residual → correctly classified stop (limit point under force control), not proof of collapse. The bounded batch DID distinguish the explanations: E2 (genuine equilibrium at partial lambda) with the plateau explained by a constant collapse load. No repair needed, no regression test required for a solver defect (there is no defect). But should I add a regression test? The assignment says \"Invalid completion, large residual or mixed trial/committed state => repair cause and add regression.\" None of those occurred. So no repair needed.\n\nBudget: 528.8s used, 3071.2 remaining, reserve 900 → 2171.2 available for exploration.\n\nNow deliverable 3: EP isolation geometry, direct and interpolated edge coupling; direct beam 2 elements, interpolated 3. Pre-yield -2k/node at lambda 1, plastic strain zero, match elastic control. DC uz=-0.20 at original tip probe: num_steps 12, max_iterations 40, tolerance 1e-8; complete, target error ≤1e-8 m, positive plastic strain. Split at -0.10, resume to -0.20, compare with uninterrupted. Verify rejected steps/cancellation leave caller restart inputs and accepted snapshots intact. Independent relative residual ≤1e-7, existing constraint tolerances, balanced support reactions.\n\nWait — \"existing EP isolation geometry\": the ep_isolation geometry is L=1.0, W=0.24, t=0.012, NX=2, NY=1, R=(0,0,0.04), CS 10x40mm beam. \"direct beam has 2 elements and interpolated counterpart 3\": so beam along the shell edge y=-W/2 (the coupled nodes are at iy=0). Direct: beam nodes coincide with shell nodes (NX=2 shell elements along x → 3 shell nodes on the edge; beam with 2 elements = 3 nodes → direct CoupledBeamShellElement at each). Interpolated: beam with 3 elements = 4 nodes, one at x=0.5 not on a shell node → InterpolatedBeamShellMPCElement.\n\nHmm wait, but the ep_isolation model couples beam nodes to shell nodes at iy=0 (y=-W/2), i.e., along the edge. \"direct and interpolated edge coupling\" — yes, edge coupling along y=-W/2.\n\nSo I need a new diagnostic script `ep_edge_coupling.py` (deliverable 3) that:\n1. Builds the EP isolation geometry (same L, W, t, R, CS, S355 curve) with:\n   - direct: NX=2 shell elements (3 edge nodes), beam 2 elements (3 nodes), CoupledBeamShellElement at each beam node.\n   - interpolated: same shell, beam 3 elements (4 nodes: x=0, 1/3, 2/3, 1), the middle two use InterpolatedBeamShellMPCElement with bilinear weights (edge nodes at iy=0; x=1/3 falls between shell nodes 0 and 0.5 → interpolated between two edge shell nodes... wait NY=1 means only 2 rows; the edge y=-W/2 has nodes at ix=0,1,2 (x=0,0.5,1). Beam node at x=1/3: between shell nodes ix=0 (x=0) and ix=1 (x=0.5). Bilinear weights on the edge: masters = grid[(0,0)] and grid[(1,0)] with weights (1-2/3, 2/3)? x=1/3 → sx = (1/3)/0.5 = 2/3 → ix=1? Hmm: sx = xb/(L/nx) = (1/3)/(0.5) = 0.667 → ix = 0, xix = 0.667. masters grid[(0,0)] w=0.333, grid[(1,0)] w=0.667. Good, genuinely interpolated.\n   \n   Actually wait — should the interpolated beam have 3 elements over the same length? \"direct beam has 2 elements and interpolated counterpart 3\". Yes.\n\n2. Pre-yield -2k/node: both direct and interpolated, plastic (scale=1.0), must complete at lambda=1, plastic strain zero, and match elastic control (scale=1e4) uz.\n\n3. DC uz=-0.20 at original tip probe: displacement control on... which model? \"DC uz=-0.20 at original tip probe: num_steps12, max_iterations40, tolerance1e-8; complete, target error<=1e-8m, positive plastic strain.\" The \"original tip probe\" is the shell tip node (probe = grid[(NX,0)] in ep_isolation, i.e., loaded shell node at x=L, y=-W/2). Displacement control target uz=-0.20 at that node. Which coupling — direct or interpolated? It says \"Use existing EP isolation geometry and S355 curve for direct and interpolated edge coupling; direct beam has 2 elements and interpolated counterpart 3. Pre-yield -2k/node must complete at lambda1... DC uz=-0.20 at original tip probe...\" I'll run DC on both direct and interpolated (or at least one; to be safe, both — but budget). The split/restart comparison: \"Split at uz=-0.10, resume to -0.20 and compare u/lambda/history with uninterrupted using existing restart tolerances, no relaxation.\"\n\nLet me first check the DisplacementControl API and restart API. Let me read the DisplacementControl class and how restart checkpoints work (emit_restart_checkpoint, restart_checkpoint param).\n\nAlso \"Verify rejected steps/cancellation leave caller restart inputs and accepted snapshots intact\" — need a cancellation token test: start a solve with a cancellation token cancelled mid-run (or cancel via callback), verify the result's restart inputs (the checkpoint the caller passed?) and accepted snapshots are intact. Hmm, \"caller restart inputs\" — when you pass restart_checkpoint=... to resume, a rejected step/cancellation must leave the caller's restart input unmodified, and accepted snapshots intact. I'll design: run DC to -0.10 with emit_restart_checkpoint=True → checkpoint1. Resume from checkpoint1 with a cancellation token that cancels after first step → verify solve aborts cleanly, checkpoint1 (caller input) unchanged deep-equal, and the result's accepted snapshots/steps correspond to committed steps only. Then resume again from checkpoint1 to -0.20 without cancellation → compare with uninterrupted.\n\nLet me read the DisplacementControl class and restart handling.", "summary": []}
