n        resumed_load,\r\n        control=\"displacement\",\r\n        displacement_control=full_control,\r\n        num_steps=2,\r\n        restart_checkpoint=first.restart_checkpoint_bytes(),\r\n        **_static_kwargs(),\r\n    )\r\n\r\n    assert uninterrupted.status == resumed.status == \"completed\"\r\n    np.testing.assert_array_equal(resumed.displacements, uninterrupted.displacements)\r\n    assert [step.to_dict() for step in resumed.steps] == [\r\n        step.to_dict() for step in uninterrupted.steps\r\n    ]\r\n    assert resumed.restart_checkpoint_bytes() == uninterrupted.restart_checkpoint_bytes()\r\n\r\n\r\ndef test_accelerated_displacement_dispatch_delegates_checkpoint_semantics(monkeypatch) -> None:\r\n    from anysolver import nonlinear_performance\r\n\r\n    nonlinear_performance.install_nonlinear_performance_optimizations()\r\n    original = nonlinear_performance._ORIGINAL_DISPLACEMENT_SOLVER\r\n    assert original is not None\r\n    calls: list[bool] = []\r\n", "numLines": 50, "startLine": 404, "requestedOffset": 404, "requestedLimit": 50, "totalLines": null, "wasTruncated": true}, "outputText": "", "durationMs": 0.0, "display": {"success": true, "verb": "Read", "message": "50 lines from C:\\Github\\ANYsolver\\tests\\test_nonlinear_restart_checkpoint.py", "warnings": [], "approvalNote": null, "suffix": "(truncated)"}, "decision": "execute", "approvalType": "always", "approvalSource": "bypass"}}
{"id": "reasoning-3033ad4e-1693-55ab-a85a-3893ca5087b1-0", "sessionId": "b9a80757-48a2-a90d-2f72-692761706b93", "turnId": "turn-8c7e0354138e253f073f636b837c66de", "createdAt": 1792346342974, "updatedAt": 1792346342974, "generationStatus": "completed", "relatedEntryId": null, "type": "reasoning", "text": "The restart pattern is clear: solve to half target with emit_restart_checkpoint, then resume with full_control and restart_checkpoint=first.restart_checkpoint_bytes(). The resumed steps must equal uninterrupted steps exactly.\n\nNow write the deliverable-3 diagnostic script `ep_edge_coupling.py`:\n\nModels (EP isolation geometry: L=1.0, W=0.24, t=0.012, NX=2, NY=1, R=(0,0,0.04), CS with fiber_plasticity, S355 curve):\n- `direct`: shell 2x1, beam 2 elements (3 nodes at x=0,0.5,1.0 on edge y=-W/2, z=+0.04), CoupledBeamShellElement at each beam node.\n- `interp`: same shell, beam 3 elements (4 nodes at x=0,1/3,2/3,1), middle nodes interpolated (weights on edge shell nodes).\n\nWait — in ep_isolation, beam nodes are placed above shell nodes at iy=0 (y=-W/2). For interpolated, beam nodes at x=1/3, 2/3 need masters from the edge row: grid[(0,0)] (x=0), grid[(1,0)] (x=0.5), grid[(2,0)] (x=1). x=1/3: between x=0 and 0.5 → weights 2/3 on grid[(1,0)]... let me compute: local coordinate xi = (xb - x_ix)/ (x_{ix+1}-x_ix) = (1/3-0)/0.5 = 2/3 → masters grid[(0,0)] w=1/3, grid[(1,0)] w=2/3. x=2/3: xi=(2/3-0.5)/0.5=1/3 → masters grid[(1,0)] w=2/3, grid[(2,0)] w=1/3.\n\nHmm, but should the interpolated coupling use bilinear weights over the 2D shell element (like convergence_study does with sy=0.5*ny → edge)? In convergence_study, beam nodes at y=0 (center) with sy=0.5*ny → for ny=1, sy=0.5, iy=0, xiy=0.5 → interpolating across the two rows. For edge coupling at iy=0 (y=-W/2), the beam node at x=1/3 lies on the edge between two edge nodes; the bilinear weights over element (ix=0, iy=0): xix=2/3, xiy=0 → masters grid[(0,0)] w=(1/3), grid[(1,0)] w=2/3. Same as 1D linear interpolation. Good — use the same candidate logic as convergence_study but with sy at the edge (iy=0, xiy=0).\n\nActually simpler: reuse the convergence_study.build pattern but with the EP isolation geometry/CS/material. I'll write a builder in the new script.\n\nLoad: -2k/node on the loaded shell edge? In ep_isolation, loads are applied at grid[(NX, iy)] for all iy (both rows: NY+1=2 nodes at x=L). \"per loaded shell node\" — the pre-yield check: -2k/node. I'll apply fz=-2000 at each of the 2 tip nodes (like ep_isolation).\n\nProbe: \"original tip probe\" = grid[(NX, 0)] (the ep_isolation probe).\n\nChecks:\nA. Pre-yield force control: direct and interpolated, plastic (S355, scale 1.0), fz=-2000/node, num_steps=6, max_iterations=30 (original params? The assignment says for deliverable 3 pre-yield: \"Pre-yield -2k/node must complete at lambda1, plastic strain zero and match elastic control.\" Solve params: keep six steps/max_iterations=30/default tolerance for consistency). Assert: status completed, load_factor==1, alpha_max==0, max_eq_plastic_strain==0, uz matches elastic control (scale 1e4) to tight tolerance.\nB. DC: uz target -0.20 at probe node, num_steps=12, max_iterations=40, tolerance=1e-8, corotational (the rotating MPC route — kinematics corotational; the coupled model with rotating map). \"complete, target error<=1e-8m, positive plastic strain\". Run on direct (and interpolated? The deliverable says \"Use existing EP isolation geometry and S355 curve for direct and interpolated edge coupling... DC uz=-0.20 at original tip probe\". I'll run DC on both direct and interpolated to be thorough — but budget. Each DC run: 12 steps, 40 iters, tolerance 1e-8, small model (2 shell + 2-3 beam elements). The corotational plastic force runs took ~50s for 12 steps at looser tolerance. DC at 1e-8 might take longer, maybe 60-100s each. Hmm. Budget: 3071 - 900 reserve = 2171s available. Deliverable 4 needs R1-R4 (R4 ~30-40s), M1-M4, P1-P4 at 140k (M3 13s at 12k... at 140k M1-M3 took? HANDOVER: M1-M3/P1-P3 at 140k all converged; run times not given, but R3 at 12k took 13.1s; at 140k probably similar or more, maybe 20-60s each). 8 runs × ~40s ≈ 320s. Deliverable 5: analytical reference — nx8/16/32 shells × beam counts × 2 eccentricities × 2 targets... that's a lot of runs. Each is a cantilever with nx up to 32, ny2 → 64 shell elements + beams; corotational elastic. At 30/60 deg rotation with moment loading. Could be ~10-60s each. Maybe 12-16 runs ≈ 500s. Final regression: 25+ modules, 600s cap — likely uses most of 600s. Reserve 900s covers final regression + verification.\n\nSo I have roughly 2171 - (320 + 500) ≈ 1350s for deliverable 3 runs. DC runs: uninterrupted + split (2 runs per coupling) + cancellation test. Let me do DC on direct coupling (the primary), and also interpolated if budget allows. Actually the deliverable text: \"Use existing EP isolation geometry and S355 curve for direct and interpolated edge coupling; direct beam has 2 elements and interpolated counterpart 3. Pre-yield -2k/node must complete at lambda1, plastic strain zero and match elastic control. DC uz=-0.20 at original tip probe...\" — the DC spec doesn't name which coupling; the split/resstart comparison is \"compare u/lambda/history with uninterrupted\". I'll run DC on both direct and interpolated (uninterrupted + split each = 4 runs) plus cancellation (1-2 runs). If each is ~60-90s that's 300-500s. Acceptable.\n\nWait, but there's a concern: DC to uz=-0.20 on this model — is it achievable? The force-control collapse was at uz≈-0.177 (at the probe? the probe uz at collapse was -0.177). DC to -0.20 goes beyond the force-control limit point — that's exactly why displacement control is used (to pass limit points). With 12 steps and 40 iterations at 1e-8 tolerance, it should complete if the model is stable there. \"complete, target error<=1e-8m, positive plastic strain\" is the acceptance.\n\nC. Split at -0.10: solve DC to -0.10 (num_steps 6? \"Split at uz=-0.10, resume to -0.20 and compare u/lambda/history with uninterrupted using existing restart tolerances, no relaxation.\" The existing restart test uses half steps: full 4 steps, half 2 steps. Here: uninterrupted 12 steps to -0.20; split: 6 steps to -0.10, then resume with full_control (target -0.20) num_steps=6. Compare displacements, load factors (lambda history), steps history with uninterrupted. \"using existing restart tolerances\" — the same tolerance 1e-8, no relaxation.\n\nD. Rejected steps/cancellation: pass a CancellationToken, cancel during the solve (e.g., via status_callback after first step), verify: the caller's restart input (checkpoint bytes passed in) is unchanged (deep compare before/after), and the result's accepted snapshots (record_increment_snapshots=True) correspond to committed steps only, and result.restart_checkpoint reflects last committed step. Also for a fresh solve with cancellation: accepted snapshots intact (each snapshot is a committed increment).\n\nLet me check CancellationToken API in control.py.", "summary": []}
